[{:seon.task/name "a-first-fork-waits-for-nothing-it-does-not-use"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-08: \"Slow tests should be treated as bugs and fixed\". Owner: runtime. What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: the boot server loads main's tests at its start (seon.fork-server/main, fork_server.clj:354, program/load-all!); root's boot asks :preload once its import is written, and seon.fork-server/preload (fork_server.clj:150) does tests, graph-at-fork, index-at-fork, warm-start (fork_server.clj:77) and lib/static! as ONE step; a :fork that arrives first runs the same pieces inline (the :fork arm, fork_server.clj:205-238). Measured 2026-11-10: a root's first agent waits 4.5 s (preload! 1.2, test load 1.1, reach graph 0.8, text index 0.7); later forks 20-54 ms; under load the warm-up ran ~35 s at ~98% CPU, holding the inbox. Cause: warm-up is one step that holds the server's inbox, and a fork that comes first pays all of it whether it uses it or not. Design: the server queues each warm piece as a message to itself (decode, tests by namespace, graph, index, text index, static) and serves a frame from root before any warm step, so no request waits longer than one piece; an agent forked before a piece is warm makes that piece itself on first use (seon.agent/base-graph agent.clj:771, reconcile-index :836, its prove's test load), derived data regenerable from the db (docs/data-modeling.md#derived-data-is-a-cache-never-relied-on). Under the one start and one derivation, the reach graph and the test load are keyed by program digest and the graph may go entirely once recorded reads replace it; this task keeps only the scheduling. Same cause as seon.fork-server/a-commit-is-applied-while-the-fork-server-warms (merge them). Approved apart (Point, 2026-11-10): warm-start's per-agent part moved off the fork path, on condition seon.agent-test/an-agents-start-costs-the-same-however-long-its-root-has-run stays green with its bound unmoved. Estimate: a root's first agent ready within a fork (~70 ms) plus its own start, flat in the program's size. Proofs, red first: seon.fork-server-test/root-s-first-agent-ready-within-fork-s; seon.agent-test/an-agent-holds-mains-tests-and-runs-them (stays green)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fork-server-test/root-s-first-agent-ready-within-fork-s seon.agent-test/an-agent-holds-mains-tests-and-runs-them}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-answer-pairs-by-request-and-stop-ends-a-wedged-server"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: (1) :stats, :static and :preload answers pair with :waiters by FIFO (peek, fork_server.clj:532 and :553); the request is sent with its :rid stripped (fork_server.clj:528), so the server cannot echo it; after a promote the standby is sent every unanswered request again (fork_server.clj:479), and an answer cut by the dead server's EOF pairs the next waiter with the wrong request. (2) release! (fork_server.clj:427) now ends the boot server's whole process group with SIGKILL before its blocking c-waitpid (lib.clj:53), so a wedged boot server no longer hangs a stop; a promoted server leads no group and is left to read its EOF; then the event reader is joined with .join 5000 (fork_server.clj:450), a clock deciding. Cause: three request-id schemes for one pattern (:rid/:tag, fx's :dest, the agent's :k); this one has none. Design: the request carries :seon.step/request-id to the server and the answer echoes it; the proc answers the waiter by id (step/pend and answered); a promoted server is killed by its pid, not left to its EOF; the reader's join waits for its EOF with no timeout (the kill guarantees it). Deletes: FIFO pairing and the .join timeout. Estimate: O(1) per answer. Proofs, red first: seon.procs-test/a-promoted-servers-answers-pair-by-request-id; seon.procs-test/stop-ends-a-wedged-fork-server (a promoted server that reads nothing); design guard seon.main-test/an-answer-pairs-by-its-request-id (no proc pairs answers by queue order)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/stop-ends-a-wedged-fork-server seon.procs-test/a-promoted-servers-answers-pair-by-request-id seon.main-test/an-answer-pairs-by-its-request-id}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tx-step-that-threw-on-a-map-says-which"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. Seen once 2026-11-05 (standing store, before an erase), not reproduced: the fork server's step threw on :tx, nth not supported on PersistentArrayMap. What exists [M]: the :tx arm (fork_server.clj:178-189) maps (nth op 1) and (nth op 2) over the message's :tx-data for seon.doc/carry-index! (doc.clj:615), assuming each op is a vector; the writer's notice is typed :seon.db/resolved-tx (a vector of [op e a v]) in resources/seon/schemas/seon.core.edn; the step's throw is answered as :step-failed and the server serves on, so that commit's application here (and the text index carry) is lost until the next. Cause [H]: some sender puts a map in :tx-data and no contract checks the message where it is made. Design: the message from the writer to the fork server is one schema (:seon.fork-server/tx, its :tx-data :seon.db/resolved-tx), checked where the writer makes it (seon.writer), so a map is refused at its producer naming it; find the producer by running that check over a day's commits. Estimate: one validation per commit at the writer (~O(datoms)). Proof, red first: seon.fork-server-test/commit-tx-data-holds-map-entry-refused-where (a writer commit whose tx-data holds a map is refused at the writer, naming it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fork-server-test/commit-tx-data-holds-map-entry-refused-where}}
{:seon.task/name "an-agent-inherits-the-section-leaves-it-searches"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: a measurement and a proposal to the owner, no build. Owner: runtime. Measured 2026-11-08 (seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-search-reads-the-index-it-inherited): an agent's first grep allocates 8-15 MB more than its second, all cold decoding of the EAVT leaves holding sections' :seon.doc/text, which every forked agent decodes for itself; the text index itself is inherited current. Candidate: read those leaves in the fork server's warm steps (seon.fork-server/warm-start, fork_server.clj:77), so agents inherit them decoded (copy-on-write). Measure first: the fork server's resident memory with and without (docs/machine.md's budget) against bytes saved per agent times agents per hour; propose with the numbers. Folds into the warm-step queue of seon.fork-server/a-first-fork-waits-for-nothing-it-does-not-use if accepted. Estimate: saves 8-15 MB x agents forked; costs the leaves' decoded size once in the server. Proof once accepted: seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-search-allocates-what-its-second-does."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-search-allocates-what-its-second-does}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fork-step-that-throws-answers-every-waiting-ask"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. Found 2026-11-09: a :fork step threw, root recorded :seon.fork-server/step-failed, :seon.agent/failed and :seon.agent/died, and the in-agent attach waiting on the agent the fork owed was never answered (a shard 15+ min at 0% CPU). What exists [M]: the server's step catches any throw and sends {:ev :step-failed :op :agent} (fork_server.clj:161-168, :298-301); root's api records it as a :platform problem only (system.clj:839); the spawn is ended only by :fork-failed (spawn-failed, system.clj:833-835), which a :step-failed on a :fork never triggers; answer-taken does end a lost :fork's spawn (system.clj:992-993) but only for a throw in root's own step. Cause: a fork step's failure is two events with two meanings, and only one ends the spawn. Design: a :step-failed whose :op is :fork is the fork's refusal: root answers the spawn (spawn-failed with the error), which answers every ask waiting on that agent, as seon.repl/defect-answer does for render (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs); one event, :fork-failed carrying the error, and :step-failed is for steps that owe no one. Estimate: none added. Proof, red first: seon.fork-server-test/fork-step-throws-answers-attach-waiting-agent-error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fork-server-test/fork-step-throws-answers-attach-waiting-agent-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-commit-is-applied-while-the-fork-server-warms"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. Seen 2026-11-11: \"fork lag 23952 ms\" on an idle system: the boot's 10 commits queued behind the fork server's warm-up and were applied in order as it drained; the tile's p50 over a 200-commit window then stays frozen at that backlog. What exists [M]: :applied's :lag-ms is now minus the writer's :ns at its send (fork_server.clj:203-204); root keeps a 200-entry window (system.clj:836); the warm-up is one :preload step (fork_server.clj:150, :239-243) during which the server's inbox is not read. Cause: the same as seon.fork-server/a-first-fork-waits-for-nothing-it-does-not-use (warm-up holds the inbox); and the stat mixes boot backlog with steady state. Design: the warm-up queued as steps between requests (that task), so a :tx is applied before the next warm step; the tile reads lag of commits applied after the server reported warm. Merge into that task. Estimate: a commit made during warm-up applied within its own apply time (ms), not the warm-up's (s). Proof, red first: seon.fork-server-test/a-commit-made-while-the-fork-server-warms-is-applied-at-once."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fork-server-test/a-commit-made-while-the-fork-server-warms-is-applied-at-once}}
]
